       "The Kadwak (Padmavat's Nagmati-viyoga section) is the most poignant part of Malik Muhammad Jayasi's immortal Padmavat (1540 CE) — it portrays the separation-pangs of Nagmati, Ratnasen's first wife, who expresses her grief as a Barahmasa when her husband leaves for Simhaldvipa",
       "Jayasi's full name is Malik Muhammad Jayasi — the epithet 'Jayasi' comes from being a resident of Jayas (Raebareli, UP); he is the finest poet of the Sufi prem-margi branch and belongs to Bhaktikal's Nirgun stream",
       "Padmavat was composed in 1540 CE (Hijri 947) — it is an epic in Awadhi, in Doha-Chaupai metre, based on the love of Raja Ratnasen for Padmavati, princess of Simhala",
       "Kadwak means a poetic section built from alternating Doha-Chaupai stanzas; Padmavat is divided into many Kadwaks, and the textbook contains the one describing Nagmati's separation",
