       "Exam fact — Tulsi 1532–1623; 'Ramcharitmanas'; the synthesist poet.",
       "Message — exclusive devotion, maryada and people's welfare are life's ideal.",
       "Literary importance — 'Ramcharitmanas', Hindi's greatest epic.",
       "Tulsidas (1532–1623 CE) is Bhaktikal's finest poet of the Saguna current's Rama-bhakti branch — called the 'Loknayak'; initiated into the Rama-mantra by his guru Narharidas, he carried Rama-bhakti to every home",
       "Tulsidas's birth is placed around 1532 CE at Rajapur village in Banda (UP) — orphaned in childhood and enduring dire poverty, he became a mahakavi through study and discipline; his jayanti is observed on Shravana Shukla Saptami",
       "Tulsidas's immortal work 'Ramcharitmanas' (begun 1574 CE, Ayodhya) is an epic in Awadhi, in Doha-Chaupai metres — seven kandas (Bala, Ayodhya, Aranya, Kishkindha, Sundara, Lanka, Uttara), based on Valmiki's Ramayana; it is called the 'Lokaveda'",
       "Tulsidas's other chief works — Vinaypatrika (Brajbhasha, the peak of dainya-bhakti), Kavitavali (Kavitt-Savaiya metres), Dohavali (niti-bhakti dohas), Gitavali, Barvai Ramayan, Ramlala Nahachhu, Vairagya Sandipani, Janaki Mangal, Parvati Mangal, Ramagya Prashna, Hanuman Bahuk",
       "Tulsi is a 'Samanvayavadi' poet — he harmonised every opposition: saguna-nirguna, jnana-bhakti, Shaiva-Vaishnava, man-woman, Brahmana-Shudra; 'Siyaramamaya saba jaga jani' is his all-seeing creed",
       "Tulsi's bhakti is of 'dasya bhava' — he deems himself Rama's servant; Hanuman is his ideal — the symbol of service, fidelity and surrender; navadha bhakti (shravana, kirtana etc.) too is expounded in the Manas",
       "Tulsi's Rama is 'Maryada Purushottama' — Vishnu's avatara, dharma's protector, the ideal son-husband-king; unlike Valmiki's man-best Rama, Tulsi's Rama is Parabrahman — this is Saguna bhakti's base",
       "Tulsi's tongue is chiefly Awadhi (Manas, Barvai), yet he is equally masterful in Brajbhasha (Vinaypatrika, Kavitavali) — Sanskrit-tinged, adorned with idioms and proverbs, tied to both folk and shastra",
       "Niti holds a vital place in Tulsi's poetry — Dohavali's niti-dohas are still used like proverbs; satsanga, compassion, service and sharanagati are his life-philosophy's pillars",
       "Tulsi's age was Mughal-era social disintegration — amid caste rigidity and religious ostentation Tulsi preached equality in bhakti through the Shabari-Nishada episodes; he is also linked with the Ramlila tradition",
       "Tulsi's poetry holds deep karuna and sharanagati — Vinaypatrika's padas are the peak of dainya and self-surrender; padas like 'Mo sama dina na dina hita' are the devotee-heart's cry",
       "Tulsidas's poetry stays relevant today — the Manas is read daily in Hindu homes; its ideals of maryada, sacrifice, service and harmony inspire modern society too",
